(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-pulmonary-anatomy-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pulmonary Anatomy — Anatomy Exam 2 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How many lobes does the right lung have, versus the left lung?","choices":["Right lung has two lobes (superior, inferior); left lung has three lobes (superior, middle, inferior)","Both lungs have an identical number of lobes, with three lobes each (superior, middle, inferior)","Neither lung is divided into lobes; both function as single undivided respiratory units","Right lung has three lobes (superior, middle, inferior); left lung has two lobes (superior, inferior)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The right lung has three lobes (superior, middle, and inferior), while the left lung has two lobes (superior and inferior). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What fissures does the right lung have, versus the left lung?","choices":["Both lungs have identical fissure patterns, each with one horizontal and one oblique fissure","Neither lung has any fissures; both remain completely undivided structures","Right lung has a horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure; left lung has only an oblique fissure","Right lung has only an oblique fissure; left lung has both a horizontal and oblique fissure"],"answer":2,"exp":"The right lung has a horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure (separating its three lobes), while the left lung has only the oblique fissure (separating its two lobes). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the cardiac notch, and what is the lingula?","choices":["Both structures are found identically in both lungs, each accommodating the heart's position within the thorax equally","Cardiac notch is found in the right lung only, accommodating the liver; lingula is a fold of the right lung's middle lobe","Neither structure is related to the heart's position; both instead relate to lymphatic drainage of the mediastinum","Cardiac notch is an indentation in the left lung accommodating the heart; lingula is a tongue-like structure from the superior lobe wrapping around the heart"],"answer":3,"exp":"The left lung has a cardiac notch (an indentation accommodating the heart) and a groove for the aorta; the lingula is a tongue-like structure coming out of the superior lobe that wraps around the heart. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the difference between extrapulmonary and intrapulmonary bronchi?","choices":["Neither term is related to bronchial anatomy; both instead describe separate divisions of the pulmonary artery branches","Both terms describe the trachea specifically, distinguishing its cervical from its thoracic portion","Extrapulmonary bronchi are inside the lungs; intrapulmonary bronchi are outside (distinct, unrelated branching structures)","Extrapulmonary bronchi are outside the lungs; intrapulmonary bronchi are inside the lungs (same branching structures, named by location)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The primary bronchi are called extrapulmonary bronchi while outside the lungs, and intrapulmonary bronchi once inside the lungs, where they branch numerous times into secondary and tertiary bronchi. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the diameter range of bronchioles, and why don't they require cartilage plates?","choices":["10-15 mm diameter; they rely mainly on cartilage rings rather than smooth muscle tone","0.3-0.5 mm diameter; they are self-supporting and therefore don't require cartilage plates","0.3-0.5 mm diameter; they require cartilage plates identical to the trachea's C-shaped rings","5-10 mm diameter; they require cartilage plates similar to the larger primary bronchi"],"answer":1,"exp":"Bronchioles have a diameter of 0.3-0.5 mm; they are self-supporting and therefore do not require cartilage plates (unlike the larger bronchi), consisting instead of smooth muscle for bronchodilation and bronchoconstriction. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What causes bronchodilation versus bronchoconstriction of the bronchioles' smooth muscle, and what nerve senses irritants/pain there?","choices":["Sympathetic stimulation causes bronchoconstriction; parasympathetic stimulation causes bronchodilation; the phrenic nerve senses irritants and pain","The bronchioles have no sensory innervation at all; only the trachea carries pain and irritant sensory fibers","Neither autonomic division affects bronchiole smooth muscle; diameter is fixed and unresponsive to nervous input","Sympathetic stimulation causes bronchodilation; parasympathetic stimulation causes bronchoconstriction; the vagus nerve senses irritants and pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation causes bronchodilation, while parasympathetic stimulation causes bronchoconstriction of the bronchioles' smooth muscle; irritants and pain are sensed by the vagus nerve. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What are type I pneumocytes (alveolar cells), and where does gas exchange occur?","choices":["Cuboidal cells that secrete surfactant into the alveolar space","Cartilage cells that support and stiffen the alveolar wall","Squamous cells lining the alveoli, where gas exchange takes place","Macrophages that phagocytize debris within the alveolar space"],"answer":2,"exp":"Type I pneumocytes (alveolar cells) are the squamous cells lining the alveoli in which gas exchange takes place; the alveolar lining consists of a single layer of these squamous cells. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What do type II pneumocytes secrete, and what is the function of this secretion?","choices":["Secrete mucus, which traps and clears inhaled debris","Secrete hormones that regulate blood pressure","Secrete digestive enzymes for local protein breakdown","Secrete surfactant, which prevents alveolar collapse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Type II pneumocytes, scattered among the type I pneumocytes, secrete surfactant, which prevents alveolar collapse. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What are alveolar macrophages (dust cells), and what do they do?","choices":["Cartilage cells that provide rigid structural support within the bronchiole walls","Cells that move freely within the alveoli, phagocytizing bacteria, dead cells, and foreign bodies","Squamous cells that form the thin alveolar wall where gas exchange occurs","Cells that produce surfactant, preventing alveolar collapse during exhalation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Alveolar macrophages (dust cells) move freely within the alveoli, phagocytizing (engulfing and digesting) bacteria, dead cells, and foreign bodies. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is the sequence of gas exchange at the alveoli, from pulmonary arteries to pulmonary veins?","choices":["Only CO2 is exchanged at the alveoli; oxygen exchange instead occurs separately in the peripheral tissue capillaries, bypassing the alveolar sacs and pulmonary circulation entirely","Pulmonary arteries transport CO2 to alveolar capillaries → CO2 leaves capillaries into alveolar sacs → O2 leaves alveolar sacs into capillaries → O2 enters pulmonary veins, returning to the heart","Pulmonary veins transport CO2 to the alveolar capillaries → CO2 leaves into the alveolar sacs → O2 leaves the sacs into the veins → O2 enters the pulmonary arteries, returning to the heart","Gas exchange occurs directly within the pulmonary arteries themselves as blood travels toward the lungs, entirely bypassing the alveolar capillaries and sacs altogether"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pulmonary arteries transport carbon dioxide to the alveolar capillaries; carbon dioxide leaves the capillaries and enters the alveolar sacs; oxygen leaves the alveolar sacs and enters the capillaries; oxygen then enters the pulmonary veins and returns to the heart to be pumped to all parts of the body. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Approximately how many alveoli does each lung have, and what surrounds each alveolus to maintain its shape?","choices":["About 1,000 alveoli; cartilage rings surround each alveolus to maintain its rigid shape","About 150 million alveoli; elastic tissue surrounds each alveolus to maintain shape and position during breathing","About 150 million alveoli; no surrounding tissue maintains their shape, which instead relies on internal pressure alone","About 1 million alveoli; smooth muscle alone maintains their shape throughout the breathing cycle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each lung has about 150 million alveoli, surrounded by an extensive network of capillaries; elastic tissue surrounds each alveolus, maintaining its shape and position during inhalation and exhalation. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What do the bronchial arteries supply, and are the alveoli/respiratory bronchioles dependent on them for oxygen?","choices":["Bronchial arteries supply the entire lung uniformly, with no distinction made between the bronchi and the alveolar tissue","The alveoli depend entirely on the bronchial arteries for their oxygen supply, receiving none from inspired air directly","Bronchial arteries supply the bronchi and connective tissue of the lungs; the alveoli and respiratory bronchioles are self-sufficient at oxygenation","Bronchial arteries supply only the alveoli directly; the bronchi instead receive no dedicated blood supply of their own"],"answer":2,"exp":"The bronchial arteries supply the bronchi and connective tissue of the lungs; the alveoli and respiratory bronchioles are self-sufficient at oxygenation (via direct gas exchange with inspired air, not requiring bronchial artery-delivered blood for their own oxygen needs). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Where do the bronchial arteries originate from, and what is the most common anatomical variation?","choices":["Originate from the coronary arteries directly, near vertebral level T5-T6, with a fixed pattern of two vessels on each side","Originate from the abdominal aorta near vertebral level L1-L2; there is no described anatomical variation among individuals","Originate from the proximal descending thoracic aorta around T5-T6; 40% (majority) of the population have one right and two left bronchial arteries","Originate from the pulmonary trunk near its bifurcation; all individuals have an identical two right and two left bronchial artery pattern"],"answer":2,"exp":"The bronchial arteries originate from the proximal descending thoracic aorta, around the level of T5 or T6; there are four anatomical variations, with 40% (the majority) of the population having one right bronchial artery and two left bronchial arteries. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What is the visceral pleura, and what is the parietal pleura?","choices":["Visceral pleura covers the outer surface of the lung; parietal pleura covers the inside lining of the thoracic wall","Visceral pleura covers the inside of the thoracic wall; parietal pleura covers the outer surface of the lung","Neither pleural layer is a serous membrane; both consist instead of dense fibrous connective tissue","Both pleural layers cover identical structures, each lining only the outer surface of the lungs directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"The visceral pleura portion covers the outer surface of the lung, while the parietal pleura portion covers the inside lining of the thoracic wall; together they form the continuous serous membrane lining each lung. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What separates the right and left pleural cavities, and how much pleural fluid does the pleural cavity normally contain?","choices":["Separated by the diaphragm; contains 500 mL of pleural fluid","Separated by the sternum; contains no fluid normally present","Not separated at all; they form one continuous cavity","Separated by the mediastinum; contains 5-10 mL of pleural fluid"],"answer":3,"exp":"The right and left pleural cavities are separated by the mediastinum; the pleural cavity between the visceral and parietal pleura normally contains 5-10 mL of pleural fluid (serous fluid). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What is the function of pleural fluid?","choices":["Produces surfactant for the alveoli during each breath","Reduces friction when the lungs move upon inhalation and exhalation","Has no functional role; it is simply a passive metabolic byproduct","Transports oxygen directly into the underlying lung tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pleural fluid reduces friction when the lungs move during inhalation and exhalation. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What are the primary respiratory muscles for inhalation, and what nerve innervates the diaphragm?","choices":["The vagus nerve innervates the diaphragm and the internal intercostals","Diaphragm (innervated by the phrenic nerve, C3-C5) and external intercostals","Internal intercostals and the sternocleidomastoid muscles only","Only accessory muscles exist, with no primary muscles for inhalation"],"answer":1,"exp":"The primary respiratory muscles are the diaphragm (innervated by the phrenic nerve, C3-C5) and the external intercostals; internal intercostals are also involved in respiration (for exhalation). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"What do the diaphragm's contraction and relaxation cause, respectively?","choices":["The diaphragm's movement has no measurable effect on overall breathing mechanics or lung volume changes","Contraction (diaphragm moves superiorly) causes exhalation; relaxation (moves inferiorly) causes inhalation","Both contraction and relaxation cause identical increases in thoracic cavity volume each time","Contraction (diaphragm moves inferiorly) causes inhalation; relaxation (diaphragm moves superiorly) causes exhalation"],"answer":3,"exp":"The diaphragm contracts (moving inferiorly) to cause inhalation, and relaxes (moving superiorly) to cause exhalation. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What do external intercostals do, versus internal intercostals?","choices":["External intercostals elevate the ribs to aid inhalation; internal intercostals depress the ribs to aid exhalation","Neither muscle group affects rib position; both instead act solely on lung tissue elasticity","Both muscle groups perform an identical function, elevating the ribs during inhalation only","External intercostals depress the ribs for exhalation; internal intercostals elevate the ribs for inhalation"],"answer":0,"exp":"External intercostals elevate the ribs to aid in inhalation, while internal intercostals depress the ribs to aid in exhalation. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What structures does the diaphragm attach to, and what is its overall shape/position?","choices":["A large, dome-shaped skeletal muscle attaching to the lower ribs, sternum (xiphoid process), and lumbar vertebrae; separates the thoracic cavity from the abdominal cavity","A thin, cartilaginous structure attaching only to the sternum and ribs; it separates the pleural cavity from the pericardial cavity","A flat, sheet-like smooth muscle attaching only to the ribs and clavicle; it separates the two pleural cavities from each other","Attaches only to the vertebral column, with no rib or sternal attachment; it separates the abdominal cavity into left and right halves"],"answer":0,"exp":"The diaphragm is a large, dome-shaped skeletal muscle that separates the thoracic cavity from the abdominal cavity, serving as the floor of the thoracic cavity and roof of the abdominal cavity; it attaches to the lower ribs, sternum (xiphoid process), and lumbar vertebrae, and is the primary muscle of respiration. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What are the three natural openings (hiatuses) of the diaphragm, and at what vertebral levels?","choices":["Inferior vena cava (T12), esophagus (T8), and aorta (T10)","Only two hiatuses exist: esophagus and aorta","The diaphragm has no natural openings; all structures pass around it","Inferior vena cava (T8), esophagus (T10), and aorta (T12)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The diaphragm has three natural openings (hiatuses): the inferior vena cava at T8, the esophagus at T10, and the aorta at T12. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Besides its respiratory role, what other bodily processes does the diaphragm assist with by increasing/decreasing thoracic and abdominal pressure?","choices":["Only digestion of food, unrelated to pressure changes","Only urination, with no other described processes","The diaphragm has no function besides breathing","Sneezing, vomiting, defecating, and childbirth"],"answer":3,"exp":"The diaphragm helps with increasing or decreasing thoracic and abdominal pressure as needed for processes like sneezing, vomiting, defecating, and childbirth. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What are examples of accessory respiratory muscles for inhalation?","choices":["The diaphragm and external intercostals (the primary muscles, not accessory)","Scalene muscles, pectoralis minor, sternocleidomastoid, and serratus anterior","Internal intercostals, transversus thoracis, and rectus abdominis muscles only","Internal oblique, external oblique, and rectus abdominis muscles together"],"answer":1,"exp":"Accessory respiratory muscles for inhalation include the scalene muscles, pectoralis minor, sternocleidomastoid, and serratus anterior, assisting the primary muscles (diaphragm, external intercostals) as needed. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What are examples of accessory respiratory muscles for exhalation?","choices":["Diaphragm and external intercostal muscles, which contract together to further expand the thoracic cavity during forced inhalation","Pectoralis minor, serratus anterior, and sternocleidomastoid muscles, which together elevate the rib cage","Internal oblique muscle, external oblique muscle, rectus abdominis, internal intercostal muscles, and transversus thoracis muscle","Scalene muscles and sternocleidomastoid muscles, elevating the first two ribs during forced inhalation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Accessory respiratory muscles for exhalation include the internal oblique muscle, external oblique muscle, rectus abdominis, internal intercostal muscles, and transversus thoracis muscle. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What are the three types of reflexes involved in respiration?","choices":["Only mechanoreceptor reflexes","Stretch reflexes and tendon reflexes only","Only chemoreceptor and protective reflexes, with no mechanoreceptor component","Mechanoreceptor reflexes, chemoreceptor reflexes, and protective reflexes"],"answer":3,"exp":"There are three different reflexes involved in respiration: mechanoreceptor reflexes, chemoreceptor reflexes, and protective reflexes. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What do mechanoreceptor reflexes respond to, in the context of respiration?","choices":["Changes in body temperature only","Changes in blood glucose levels","Changes in lung volume or changes in blood pressure","Changes in partial pressures of carbon dioxide and oxygen only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Mechanoreceptor reflexes respond to changes in lung volume or changes in blood pressure. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What do chemoreceptor reflexes respond to, and what do they primarily respond to changes in?","choices":["Respond to changes in lung volume and thoracic wall stretch, primarily responding to changes in arterial blood pressure","Respond to changes in partial pressures of carbon dioxide (carbonic acid) and oxygen, primarily responding to changes in pH","Respond only to mechanical stretch of the lungs during deep, forced inhalation efforts","Respond only to temperature changes in inhaled air as it passes through the nasal passages"],"answer":1,"exp":"Chemoreceptor reflexes respond to changes in the partial pressures of carbon dioxide (carbonic acid) and oxygen, primarily responding to changes in pH. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What are examples of named bronchopulmonary segments of the superior lobe, per the bronchial tree diagram?","choices":["Segments are named identically across all lobes with no distinction","Apical, posterior, and anterior segments","Only a single unnamed segment per lobe","Medial basal and lateral basal segments (inferior lobe segments only)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Bronchopulmonary segments of the superior lobe include named segments such as apical, posterior, and anterior (with variations like apicoposterior on the left side). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What are examples of named bronchopulmonary segments of the inferior lobe, per the bronchial tree diagram?","choices":["Superior, anterior basal, lateral basal, medial basal, and posterior basal segments","The inferior lobe has no distinctly named bronchopulmonary segments whatsoever","Only the apical and posterior segments, which are superior lobe segments","Only the lateral and medial segments, which are middle lobe segments"],"answer":0,"exp":"Bronchopulmonary segments of the inferior lobe include the superior, anterior basal, lateral basal, medial basal, and posterior basal segments. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What structures create grooves on the medial surfaces of the right and left lungs, respectively?","choices":["Right lung has a groove for the esophagus; left lung has a groove for the aorta","Both lungs have identical grooves for the same structure","Neither lung has any grooves on its medial surface","Right lung has a groove for the aorta; left lung has a groove for the esophagus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The right lung's medial surface has a groove for the esophagus, while the left lung's medial surface has a groove for the aorta (in addition to its cardiac notch). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 43"}]};
